1. High-Level Architecture Overview
Key Data Flows
Happy path: Order event fi tx.orders.{tenantId} fi Window Matcher pairs it with a payment event fi MATCHED
status written to transactions fi idempotency key expired from Redis.
Failure path: No payment event arrives within the 30s window fi failure published to recon.failures.{tenantId} fi AI
Diagnostic Worker consumes it, generates an embedding, runs cosine similarity against known_issues in pgvector fi
top-K matches returned fi resolution suggestion stored and surfaced via the Management API.
Architecture Tiers
The system is organized into five horizontal tiers:
• Ingestion Tier: Order DB CDC (Debezium/JDBC), Webhook Ingestor (HMAC-verified HTTP), Management API
(REST/JWT OAuth2)
• Kafka Event Bus: tx.orders.{tenantId}, tx.payments.{tenantId}, recon.failures.{tenantId}
• Reconciliation Engine: Window Matcher (30s tumbling window), Idempotency Guard (Redis SETNX + TTL)
• Database Tier: PostgreSQL partitioned by tenant — transactions table + reconciliation_failures with pgvector
HNSW index
• AI Diagnostic Worker: Embedding Generator (LangChain4j + OpenAI), Similarity Search (pgvector cosine,
top-K)
Scalability Rationale
Each tenant's Kafka topics are partitioned by transactionId (consistent hashing), ensuring ordering guarantees per
transaction while allowing horizontal consumer scaling. Java 21 virtual threads allow the reconciliation engine to block
on I/O (Redis, PostgreSQL) without exhausting OS threads — this is the primary reason for choosing Project Loom
over reactive programming, which would have imposed a more complex programming model.
2. Component Breakdown
2.1 Ingestion Service
Responsibilities: Accept transactional events from two distinct sources — internal Order DB (via CDC/JDBC) and
external Payment Gateway webhooks — validate, enrich with tenant context, and publish to Kafka.
Tech choices:
• Spring Boot 3.3 with spring-kafka. Producer configured with acks=all and enable.idempotence=true for
exactly-once delivery to the broker.
• HMAC-SHA256 verification on all incoming webhooks before any downstream processing, using a per-tenant
secret stored in Kubernetes Secrets.
• Virtual threads for the HTTP server thread pool — each webhook request gets its own virtual thread.
